From 64fa3c905ff4bb648374206f559e014d8ae764a5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: NNdroid <99177648+NNdroid@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 13:27:20 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] airoha-xpon: recover a wedged EPON LLID epoch with a real
 line re-open

Cold start on this hardware can leave the EPON MAC in an LLID epoch the OLT
never grants upstream slots for: the line registers, misses every grant one
second later and loops. Nothing inside the driver could repair that state.
The only sequence measured to work is the userspace "ip link set down;
sleep 1; up", which runs controller_stop() outside state_lock. A caller that
already holds that lock deadlocks, because controller_stop() calls
synchronize_irq() before taking it while the threaded handler owns it first.

- Add a reopen worker that owns no lock the teardown needs and reproduces the
  userspace sequence: rtnl_lock, dev_close, one second of discharge, then
  dev_open. It takes rtnl first, the only lock order ndo_open/ndo_stop and the
  rest of the driver use.
- Track ownership across the close with reopen_holding. controller_stop()
  clears stopping and the active mode, so without it a retry after a failed
  open cannot tell a line the worker closed from one netifd took down. The
  worker also retries itself, because link_work exits as soon as stopping is
  set and would otherwise leave the line down for good.
- Escalate: the first cycle after 5 s, later ones after 30 s, and the third
  also reloads the provider firmware, the one step that brings a wedged
  EN7572 back.
- Ask for the reopen as soon as the burst gate has been dark for 5 s, instead
  of waiting for three short-lived registrations to prove the same thing.

Two findings from the first cold boot that this recovery exposed:

- The EN7572 reports SYS_TX_READY 3.9 s after the gate opens and clears it
  again before the 4 s capture, while the laser is still dark: the TX power
  ADC read 0x0001 instead of the 0x466e of a lit burst. The MAC trusted that
  sample, answered the Discovery Gate in that window and lost the LLID one
  second later. Require eight consecutive 20 ms polls before publishing ready,
  and restart that run on every gate transition.
- Log the whole epoch so a future failure can be read from dmesg alone:
  registration_epoch plus a reason on every reset, the MAC-start register
  dump, each Register Request, every MPCP timeout snapshot with the rx/tx MPCP
  counters, carrier transitions and OAM channel handovers. All of it is
  dev_dbg, so it costs nothing until dynamic debug is enabled.

Bump both package releases.
